    <div class="content">
        <section class="card" id="what">
            <h2>What it is</h2>
            <p>
                Ancilla is an unofficial, personal stats viewer for Halo Infinite. It started as a way to answer
                one question, <em>how many more games until I finish my battle passes and reach Hero?</em>, and grew into
                a place to look back over your matches, medals and the people you play with.
            </p>
            <p>It runs on your own machine. There's no account, no server of ours, and nothing is shared with anyone else.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="name">
            <h2>Where the name comes from</h2>
            <p>
                In the Halo universe, it's what the Forerunners called their artificial intelligences. The term
                comes from Greg Bear's Forerunner Saga novels. Ancillas range from the monitors that look after the
                Halo rings, such as 343 Guilty Spark, to vast minds like Mendicant Bias. Humans would just say "AI".
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="data">
            <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
            <p>
                Everything comes from the same web services that power halowaypoint.com, called with your own Spartan
                token, so you only ever see what Halo Waypoint would show you.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Stats:</strong> your service record, match history and full scoreboards.</li>
                <li><strong>Skill:</strong> CSR, MMR and the matchmaker's expected kills and deaths.</li>
                <li><strong>Economy:</strong> career rank and battle pass progress.</li>
                <li><strong>Content:</strong> names and images for maps, modes, playlists, medals and ranks.</li>
                <li><strong>Profile:</strong> gamertags and gamerpics.</li>
                <li><strong>Theater films:</strong> the match recordings behind Theater mode, for a match's timeline.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="note">
                These services aren't a public, documented API. They can change without notice, and when they do,
                parts of this app may stop working until it's updated.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="privacy">
            <h2>Your token and privacy</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Your Spartan token</strong> is held in server memory for your browser session only. It's never
                    written to disk, never sent back to your browser, and goes nowhere except Halo's own services.
                    Tokens expire after a few hours anyway.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Progress snapshots</strong> (your career XP, matches played and your active pass's XP, with a
                    timestamp) are saved each time your progress loads, to power the XP-per-match rates and the Hero forecast. They're stored on this
                    machine at <code class="path">@Snapshots.DisplayPath</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Theater films</strong> are downloaded only when you open a match's timeline and choose to load
                    it (usually 9–30 MB each), and kept on this machine at <code class="path">@Films.DisplayRoot</code> so
                    they needn't be downloaded again. A film holds the gamertags of everyone in the match. Delete the
                    folder to remove them.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Log files</strong> record what the app did, to help with bug reports: each call to Halo's
                    services by service name, result and time taken (never the address, which holds IDs), and the app
                    starting and signing in. Before anything is written, tokens, player IDs, gamertags, film download
                    addresses and file paths are removed. They're kept on this machine at
                    <code class="path">@Log.DisplayFolder</code>, one file a day, for a week. The
                    <a href="diagnostics">Diagnostics</a> page shows how the services are doing and can copy a redacted
                    summary for a bug report.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Your theme choice</strong> is kept in a browser cookie.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Caching:</strong> finished matches, maps, modes, medal data and images are kept in memory while
                    the app runs, so pages load faster the second time. Restarting the app clears them.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Things your browser loads directly:</strong> map thumbnails (from Halo's content servers),
                    gamerpics (from Xbox's image servers) and the Halo and XBOX themes' fonts (from Google Fonts).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="numbers">
            <h2>How the numbers are worked out</h2>
            <dl class="defs">
                <dt>KDA</dt>
                <dd>Halo's own measure: kills plus a third of your assists, minus deaths. Stats shows the average per match.</dd>
                <dt>Per match</dt>
                <dd>On the Stats page, a total divided by every match in the chosen filter, including matches you left early.</dd>
                <dt>Medal rate</dt>
                <dd>Matches in the chosen filter divided by how many times you've earned the medal, shown as "1 every N matches". Medals earned more than once a match show as "about N a match".</dd>
                <dt>PvP stats</dt>
                <dd>
                    The services can show one game mode at a time but can't leave one out, so PvP figures are your full
                    service record minus your Firefight record. Counts subtract exactly. Accuracy and average KDA are
                    recalculated from what's left. Best killing spree can't be split, so it's the overall figure.
                </dd>
                <dt>Career XP per match</dt>
                <dd>
                    The services don't report XP per game, so it's measured: the career XP gained between two snapshots,
                    divided by the matches played in between. On the Progress page you choose what to average over, such as
                    the last 50 matches or the last 30 days. Snapshots are only saved when that page loads, so the window
                    starts at the nearest snapshot, and needs at least 5 matches since it. Without enough history, your
                    lifetime average is used, which is only a rough guide.
                </dd>
                <dt>Pass XP per match</dt>
                <dd>
                    Measured the same way, from the active pass's XP saved in each snapshot, over the same window. Only
                    snapshots taken while that pass was active count, and it needs 5 matches since the first one. Career XP
                    comes in several times faster than pass XP, so the career figure isn't used for passes.
                </dd>
                <dt>Matches to go</dt>
                <dd>
                    XP still needed, divided by XP per match: career XP per match for Hero, pass XP per match for passes.
                    Only the active pass earns XP, so passes are finished one after another. Until pass XP per match has
                    been measured, passes show no figure.
                </dd>
                <dt>Hero forecast</dt>
                <dd>Your average career XP per day, extended in a straight line, counting days you didn't play. It's measured over the same window as XP per match. If that window is under a day long, or you've entered a figure yourself, it uses the last 60 days instead.</dd>
                <dt>Expected kills</dt>
                <dd>The matchmaker's own prediction for a player of your skill in that lobby, straight from the skill service.</dd>
                <dt>MMR gap</dt>
                <dd>Your team's matchmaking rating minus the other team's. Negative means you were the underdog.</dd>
                <dt>K/D and KDA</dt>
                <dd>K/D is kills ÷ deaths (just kills when you didn't die). KDA follows Halo's formula: kills + assists ÷ 3 − deaths.</dd>
                <dt>Firefight</dt>
                <dd>Games against AI only are left out of Trends and Insights, because kills against AI would swamp everything else.</dd>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="timeline">
            <h2>How the match timeline is worked out</h2>
            <p>
                Theater films aren't documented, so Ancilla reads them using what has been worked out so far. Every
                finding is checked against the match's scoreboard (see the Ancilla.Theater notes in the source).
            </p>
            <dl class="defs">
                <dt>Kills and deaths</dt>
                <dd>The film records each kill and death to the millisecond. A kill is followed by its victim's death, which gives who killed whom.</dd>
                <dt>"A bot or another player"</dt>
                <dd>Bots aren't in the film, and nor are players who joined late without killing, dying or earning a medal. Kills of them have no victim to show.</dd>
                <dt>Died, no killer recorded</dt>
                <dd>A suicide, or a death to a bot.</dd>
                <dt>Medals on a kill</dt>
                <dd>Each medal is matched to the same player's kill nearest in time, within half a second. Medals not tied to a kill (such as Steaktacular) show on their own line. Medals the app can't name yet are left out.</dd>
                <dt>Objective</dt>
                <dd>In Firefight, an award every 5 seconds for holding an objective. In Capture the Flag, see flag actions.</dd>
                <dt>Flag actions</dt>
                <dd>
                    The film records when a player's flag slot fills or empties, and an objective award for most flag actions,
                    but not which action it was. Ancilla follows each team's flag (at its base, carried or dropped) to tell
                    them apart: picking it up from its base is taking it, picking it up where it fell is picking up the dropped
                    flag, emptying the slot with an award is a capture and without one a drop, an award for killing the carrier
                    is stopping them, and an award while your team's flag is dropped is a return. A dropped flag nobody touches
                    is assumed to go home after 15 seconds. Checked against the scoreboard, captures, assists and returns almost
                    always match, and steals, pick-ups and carrier kills are within one or two in a match.
                </dd>
                <dt>Picked up a weapon</dt>
                <dd>
                    The film doesn't record a weapon disappearing when someone picks it up, but it does record the
                    player's weapon slot changing. Usually the weapon they gave up drops at their feet at the same moment.
                    Pickups during lives the film couldn't link to a player aren't shown, and which weapon it was isn't
                    known yet.
                </dd>
                <dt>Best streak</dt>
                <dd>Most kills in one life. It matches the scoreboard's best killing spree.</dd>
                <dt>Traded</dt>
                <dd>Kills after which the player died within 3 seconds.</dd>
                <dt>Revenge</dt>
                <dd>Kills of the player who last killed them.</dd>
                <dt>Nemesis</dt>
                <dd>The opponent who killed them most (the first one listed, on a tie).</dd>
                <dt>The map</dt>
                <dd>
                    The film records each player's position and aim many times a second. The map's grey area is
                    everywhere players went, seen from above (or the floor plan, below); which way is north isn't known. Not shown for Firefight,
                    where the AI's positions can't be told apart from players'.
                </dd>
                <dt>The floor plan</dt>
                <dd>
                    Forge maps come with a navigation mesh: the walkable floor the game's AI uses. Ancilla downloads it
                    and lines it up with the film by finding the placement that puts the most player positions on it
                    (usually 94–99%). It's drawn darker where the floor is higher, and the heatmap is trimmed to it.
                    If it doesn't line up well, or the map was built by 343 rather than in Forge, the map shows where
                    players went instead.
                </dd>
                <dt>Where players died, and kills</dt>
                <dd>
                    A death is placed at the victim's last position, and a kill's line starts at the killer's position at
                    that moment. Bot deaths, and kills by players whose life the film couldn't link to them, can't be
                    placed. The short line on each dot is which way that player was aiming, in the second before.
                </dd>
                <dt>Facing away from killer</dt>
                <dd>
                    The angle between where the victim was aiming and the direction to their killer: 0° is looking straight
                    at them, 180° is facing away. Hovering over a death or kill describes it in words: under 45° is facing
                    the killer, over 135° is having their back to them, and anything between is side-on.
                </dd>
                <dt>Kill of that life</dt>
                <dd>Counted from the killer's last death (or the start of the match), including this kill.</dd>
                <dt>Where players spent time</dt>
                <dd>
                    Each position counts until the player's next one (at most a second, so gaps in the film don't pile up in
                    one place). Time is spread a little to neighbouring squares, then squares are ranked: each of the six
                    colours, from blue to red, covers the same number of squares. Time while dead, and lives the film couldn't
                    link to a player, aren't counted. The table splits the map into thirds each way.
                </dd>
                <dt>Movement, animated</dt>
                <dd>
                    Each player's position and aim, sampled every quarter of a second and smoothed between samples, with
                    the last 3 seconds of their path. A player disappears while dead and during lives the film couldn't
                    link to them. Kills flash for a moment where the victim died. It never plays on its own, and with
                    reduced motion turned on in your system settings it leaves out the trails and the fading.
                </dd>
                <dt>Alive, dead or not in the match</dt>
                <dd>
                    The film lists the players who are alive in every packet, so the list under the animation can say who's
                    dead even when there's no position to show. A player on the map counts as alive. "Alive, position
                    unknown" is a player the film lists but doesn't place (also everyone during the intro, before anyone
                    moves). "Not in the match" is before a player first appears, or after they leave. The list loses a
                    player now and then, so the two disagree for a few percent of the time.
                </dd>
                <dt>Objective events on the map</dt>
                <dd>Where the player was at each objective event.</dd>
                <dt>Kill distance</dt>
                <dd>The median distance between a player and their victim at the moment of the kill, across the floor, in the film's own units, whose size differs from map to map. Compare it between players in a match, not between maps or with real-world distances.</dd>
            </dl>
        </section>

